  "text": "Question four. How do staging, intermediate, and mart layers differ? Staging has one model per source table: renaming, typing and light cleaning, with no business logic. Intermediate holds reusable business logic: joins and reshaping. Marts are the finished facts and dimensions that dashboards read. Each step stays small, testable, and written once. The layers also decide what a change costs. Select a model with a plus after it, and dbt builds everything downstream. From the customers staging model, that's three of the four models, all the way to the revenue dashboard. From the orders fact, it's one. So the business logic, which changes every week, sits downstream, in intermediate models and marts, where a change rebuilds less. Staging stays shaped like the source, which rarely changes.",
